    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d785">

                <pb facs="0819" n="769" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg769"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Terrell to Mr.
                            Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-02-24"><hi rendition="#italic">February 24, 1894</hi></date>.
                        (Received March 12.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 196.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I am informed that the naturalized citizens
                    of the United States who have been so long detained at Alexandretta<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d785-f1"/> have been ordered to depart from the Ottoman Empire.</p>
                <p>The man imprisoned at Yozgad,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d785-f2"/> who has been
                    referred to in former dispatches, will, I have reason to believe, soon be
                    expelled also.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">A. W. Terrell.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d785-f1" n="2" xml:id="d785-f1-content">
                <p>Mrs. Toprahanian was furnished in the summer of 1894 with a United States
                    passport, and by order of the Porte with a teskeré, or traveling passport, to
                    come from the interior of Asia Minor and embark with her two children for the
                    United States to join her husband, a resident of New York. She was stopped at
                    Alexandretta and detained there for more than two months on the ground that she
                    was a Turkish subject.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d785-f2" n="1" xml:id="d785-f2-content">
                <p>Aivazian.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
